   "file": "02-operation.md",
   "group": "Section 2 · Operation",
   "level": 3,
   "boards": [],
   "html": "<p>Schematic diagram in a bordered box. At upper left, a large outer rectangle labeled \"CASE(CHASSIS)\" enclosing an inner rectangle labeled \"MODEL 732A\". Inside the MODEL 732A box: a resistor symbol labeled \"R_O\" (732A output resistance) and a small rectangular block (representing internal circuitry) connected together, with leads running down to a junction where \"R1\" (resistor symbol) and \"C1\" (capacitor symbol) are connected in parallel between that junction and the chassis case line.<\/p>\n<p>On the right edge of the 732A box, four open-circle terminals are stacked vertically: the top two carry the output lines from R_O (HI and LO) out to the load, the third is labeled \"GUARD\", and the fourth (bottom) is the chassis/case line. Below the GUARD line, further to the right, is a circular symbol labeled \"E_C\" (source symbol, representing the potential difference between power line grounds), connected in series in the bottom ground-return path. Arrows trace the circulating current loop: leftward along the GUARD conductor, down through the GUARD terminal, rightward along the bottom ground line through E_C, and up at the far right to the load's LO node; a curved-arrow loop symbol is drawn where the GUARD conductor joins the load's LO node. At far right, a resistor labeled \"R_L\" (load resistance) connects the HI and LO lines, and the LO node continues down to a ground symbol at bottom right.<\/p>\n<p>Ground symbols (chassis ground) appear at two places: below the bottom (chassis line) open-circle terminal, and at the bottom right below the E_C/R_L return node.<\/p>\n<p>Legend below the figure, headed \"WHERE:\":<\/p>\n<ul><li>R1 C1 = Leakage Resistance &amp; Capacitance From Guard to Chassis<\/li><li>E_C = Potential Difference Between Power Line Grounds<\/li><li>R_O = 732A Output Resistance<\/li><li>R_L = Load Resistance<\/li><\/ul>",
   "text": "Schematic diagram in a bordered box. At upper left, a large outer rectangle labeled \"CASE(CHASSIS)\" enclosing an inner rectangle labeled \"MODEL 732A\". Inside the MODEL 732A box: a resistor symbol labeled \"R_O\" (732A output resistance) and a small rectangular block (representing internal circuitry) connected together, with leads running down to a junction where \"R1\" (resistor symbol) and \"C1\" (capacitor symbol) are connected in parallel between that junction and the chassis case line. On the right edge of the 732A box, four open-circle terminals are stacked vertically: the top two carry the output lines from R_O (HI and LO) out to the load, the third is labeled \"GUARD\", and the fourth (bottom) is the chassis/case line. Below the GUARD line, further to the right, is a circular symbol labeled \"E_C\" (source symbol, representing the potential difference between power line grounds), connected in series in the bottom ground-return path. Arrows trace the circulating current loop: leftward along the GUARD conductor, down through the GUARD terminal, rightward along the bottom ground line through E_C, and up at the far right to the load's LO node; a curved-arrow loop symbol is drawn where the GUARD conductor joins the load's LO node. At far right, a resistor labeled \"R_L\" (load resistance) connects the HI and LO lines, and the LO node continues down to a ground symbol at bottom right. Ground symbols (chassis ground) appear at two places: below the bottom (chassis line) open-circle terminal, and at the bottom right below the E_C/R_L return node. Legend below the figure, headed \"WHERE:\": R1 C1 = Leakage Resistance & Capacitance From Guard to Chassis E_C = Potential Difference Between Power Line Grounds R_O = 732A Output Resistance R_L = Load Resistance",
